"Every picture has edges. A painting has a frame, a photo has borders, and a page in your sketchbook stops where the paper stops. Inside those edges, the artist decides where everything goes. This is called composition. Move the main thing a little to the left, or make the empty space a little larger, and the whole picture feels different.": "ae8eca624785",
"Every picture has edges. A painting has a frame, a photo has borders, and a page in your sketchbook stops where the paper stops. Inside those edges, the artist decides where everything goes. This is called composition. Move the main thing a little to the left, or make the empty space a little larger, and the whole picture feels different. One thing artists think about is balance. Some shapes look heavier than others. A big shape looks heavier than a small one, and on light paper a dark shape looks heavier than a pale one. When the heavy parts are all on one side, the picture seems to tip over. When they are spread out well, it feels steady. There are three main kinds of balance. In symmetrical balance, the two sides are like mirror images. In asymmetrical balance, the two sides are different but still look balanced. A big pale shape near the middle can balance a small dark shape far out, just as a light child at the far end of a seesaw can balance a heavier child sitting near the middle. In radial balance, things spread out from a center, like the petals of a flower. Artists also decide what should catch your eye first. This is called emphasis, and the place you look at first is the focal point. An artist can make one area stand out by making it different from everything around it. Scientists who track people's eyes have found that faces and words pull our eyes strongly, even when we are asked to look for something else. You may have heard of the rule of thirds. Draw two lines across the picture and two lines down it, so that it is cut into nine equal parts, and put the main thing on one of the lines or where two of them cross. The name was first written down in 1797. It is a rule of thumb, not a law. Many fine pictures put the main thing right in the middle. Finally, look at what is not there. The empty space around and between things is called negative space, and it has a shape of its own. Chinese calligraphers say that the blank parts of a character should be planned as carefully as the brush strokes. A picture with lots of empty space can feel calm, and a crowded one can feel busy. Neither is wrong. It depends on what the artist wants you to feel.": "908e2efb4556",
